  "worked_solution": "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+{20}^{∘} & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & 4x+{20}^{∘} & ={300}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ={60}^{∘}-{20}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 4x & ={300}^{∘}-{20}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ 4x & ={40}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 4x & ={280}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={10}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={70}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={10}^{∘}$ and $x={70}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={100}^{∘}$ and $x={160}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x={190}^{∘}$ and $x={250}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x={280}^{∘}$ and $x={340}^{∘}.$\n\nAll other integer values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ \n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={10}^{∘},\\, & {x}_{2} & ={70}^{∘},\\, & {x}_{3} & ={100}^{∘}, & {x}_{4} & ={160}^{∘},\\, \\\\ {x}_{5} & ={190}^{∘},\\, & {x}_{6} & ={250}^{∘},\\, & {x}_{7} & ={280}^{∘}, & {x}_{8} & ={340}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
